  "content": "\\begin{abstract}\nGlobal logistics networks face mounting pressure to reduce their environmental footprint while maintaining operational efficiency. Traditional routing approaches primarily focus on minimizing travel time or cost, with carbon emissions often considered as secondary concerns or overlooked entirely. This paper presents a novel Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) networks that addresses this limitation through a comprehensive two-layer framework. Our approach integrates carbon emission optimization with real-time traffic adaptation using a multi-objective algorithm that dynamically balances environmental impact and travel time. Extensive experimental evaluation using a simulation of the Greater Bay Area transportation network demonstrates context-dependent performance across different traffic scenarios. In mixed conditions, our protocol achieves the lowest average travel time (75.2 minutes) while maintaining carbon awareness, with results showing no statistically significant difference from purely traffic-adaptive approaches (p = 0.3872). The proposed framework achieves carbon emission reductions while limiting travel time increases to approximately 1\\% compared to time-optimized routing. This research contributes to sustainable logistics by providing a flexible routing framework that allows operators to align routing strategies with specific sustainability goals and operational requirements, advancing the integration of environmental considerations into CPI networks.\n\\end{abstract}",
